syntax: parse zsh's try-always blocks

Zsh runs the always list after the try list, however that one ends
(zshmisc(1), "COMPLEX COMMANDS"):

	{ try-list } always { always-list }

Reproducer, before this change:

	$ printf '{ cmd } always { cleanup }\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:9: statements must be separated by &, ; or a newline

The new TryClause node holds both blocks and the position of "always".
The parser recognizes "always" right after the closing brace of a block,
on the same line, and the printer keeps the form, since it has no
equivalent.

This resolves the `TODO(zsh): { try-list } "always" { always-list }`.
Part of https://github.com/mvdan/sh/issues/120.

